shortAnswer: "A token is a chunk of text — roughly three-quarters of a word in English. AI models read and write in tokens, and they're the unit behind both what you pay and how much the model can hold at once. As a working rule: 1,000 tokens is about 750 words, and a page of text is roughly 500 tokens."
faq:
  - q: "How many tokens is a page?"
    a: "Around 500 for a typical A4 page of prose. A 20-page report is roughly 10,000. Spreadsheets and code run higher per visible character, because numbers and symbols split into more tokens than ordinary words."
  - q: "Do I pay for what I send as well as what I get back?"
    a: "Yes, on usage-based pricing — input and output are both counted, usually at different rates with output costing more. On a flat monthly subscription you're not counting tokens directly, but limits still apply behind the scenes."
  - q: "Why does attaching a long document cost so much?"
    a: "Because the whole document is sent with the request, and often re-sent as the conversation continues. A 100-page attachment discussed over twenty exchanges can be counted many times over. Attaching only the relevant section is both cheaper and more accurate."
  - q: "Is it worth optimising for tokens?"
    a: "Not for most businesses at typical volumes — staff time is worth far more than the difference. It becomes worth attention when you're running something automated at scale, where a small per-request saving multiplies."
  - q: "Do other languages use more tokens?"
    a: "Generally yes. English is the most efficiently tokenised, so the same meaning in another language often costs more tokens. Worth knowing if you operate across languages."
